1) For Python, since it can turn into a complete mess of twisted dependencies, I recommend downloading an environment manager like Anaconda.
The included environment.yml file has all the requirements needed to run the Python scripts. 

conda env create -f environment.yml 

You need to be in the directory with the file to do this or type in the full path to the environment.yml file. This creates
an environment called Reed_etal_2024_GIMDS. The environment comes with all the packages needed to run the scripts.

conda activate Reed_etal_2024_GIMDS

This activates that environment. For ease, I'd recommend using Spyder or some other IDE with an IPython console built in. Or, you can run from the anaconda prompt/shell:

ipython

This should give you a standalone IPython prompt. Run %cd <insert directory to unzipped archive here>. This sets your working directory.

2) The .py files are scripts and run using the IPython console. There are scripts associated with calculations and visualizations and those associated with the scikit-learn random forest testing of different things (hyperparameter optimization and feature importance) not available in Orfeo Toolbox version of random forest. 

The first file to run is the abundance calculator:

%run -i abundance.py

The -i flag stands for interactive (i.e., the file can access stored workspace variables). Next, run the f1_score calculator:

%run -i f1_scores.py

Then you can run the abundance plot:

%run -i abun_plot.py

Next, 

%run -i manual_vs_test.py

to produce Figure 4, which shows predicted versus manually mapped mineral abundance for the three thin sections with manual maps.
It is slightly different than the manuscript as colors where changed in Affinity Designer.

Make sure to decompress the four_plot_bio.7z using 7zip (free to download @ https://www.7-zip.org/download.html; a .zip file with the same contents would 20x larger in size). 
This extracts a pickle file, four_plot_bio.pkl. 

You could use the ipython '%reset magic' command to clear away the workspace before loading this >150 MB pickle file as it requires no previous variables. 

%run -i four_plot_bio.py

This plots up some biotite properties for the Figure 7 in the discussion section.

3) The next part is running the scikit-learn based random forest classifier on our three samples with test maps. First, in each sample directory 1_13a, 6_3a, and 16_2a 
decompress the .7z files containing the 7-pixel radius mean-filtered elemental intensity rasters (e.g., 6_3a_intensity.7z). Next in IPython, run

%run -i extract_values.py

This generates training data in the proper form for scikit-learn using the provided training polygons. The training data is ~100 MB, so I don't include it in this already voluminous archive. 
It may take a few minutes to do this.

Next, run

%run -i rf.py

to train the random forest models in scikit-learn. This also prints out classification reports for each sample. 

Next,

%run -i feature_importance.py

to obtain feature importance from the trained models and plot them (Figure 5). This may take a few minutes.

Next,

%run -i randomcv.py

to do the cross-validation hyperparameter optimization. This will several hours most likely on a decently fast computer; you can speed it up by changing the n_jobs flag to something higher. 
The best-scoring hyperparameters for each sample will be the output.






